Z||000005||1FST2M6||08-23-2000||02:27 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.dri-wash.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.dri-wash.com/</A>   magic in a bottle... used it on my mildly oxidized Milano red 92 Alfa Romeo 164L... took about 40 minutes to do and has lasted for more then 2 years... ONE 32oz bottle goes a LOOOOOOOONG way! i don't sell it.. just use it.. and it rocks! cool pump bottle and it coats lightly.. rub in, wipe off every month and she looks brand spankin new all the time. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChadMan||08-23-2000||02:38 PM||||After Oreif and Old Lar recommended the 3-step Meguiar's treatment (Body Scrub, then Polish, then Wax) I tried it on my 88 Coupe and was very happy with the results.  It doesn't look as good as Orief's but it really brought the shine back.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88Coupe7_1.jpg"> <P>||||reg||14
Z||000007||jmw||08-23-2000||03:17 PM||||I just bought / tried that Maguire's body scrub stuff this past weekend on my red '84. Did different little test areas all over the car and it consistently took the paint off. Did I do something wrong, or does this just mean there is no clear-coat left? [Car has not been garaged for 12 years. I am still trying to avoid an expensive paint job...] Thanks.<p>[This message has been edited by jmw (edited 08-23-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000008||Blade||08-23-2000||03:46 PM||||Mine was repainted it might not have a clear coat who knows ...will that matter?<P>Blade||||reg||1
Z||000009||SloPonti||08-24-2000||12:51 AM||||hey guys i do sell that dry wash n guard stuff that 1fast2m6 was talking about! actually i really don't try to sell it-i am mainly a distributer so that i can get it for myself-i really only sell to friends and family but i use it all the time and man it is great stuff! my car ia an 84 with orginal paint and it looks like new! check out these pics of my car!<BR>thanks <A HREF="http://members.aol.com/sloponti/fiero1.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://members.aol.com/sloponti/fiero1.jpg</A>  <A HREF="http://members.aol.com/sloponti/fiero2.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://members.aol.com/sloponti/fiero2.jpg</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Oreif||08-24-2000||02:27 AM||||Blade,<BR>I have used the Meguires on all kinds of cars, even ones without clear coats and it works fine.<P>JWM, <BR>If your car is heavily oxidized it will remove all the oxidized paint. If the car was repainted they may have used the wrong paint or painted it incorrectly. A friend had a car (not a fiero) that was red and oxidized so much it looked like it was painted with matte pink! After many hours of cleaning,polishing and waxing the car was back to a shiny red car. The guy he bought it from thought he repainted it!  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 08-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||peterh||08-24-2000||08:03 AM||||Is it possible to apply a new coat of clearcoat to an old paint job after sanding with 600 wetdry, etc.? Or will it look like crap?<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||DXR_DAD||08-24-2000||10:36 AM||||My car is a single stage BLACK, with *no clear coat* It my need a little more buffing than a clear coat paint job but I have had no problems with the Meguiers 3 step system.<BR>It shines like new money!!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> The car has been repainted but it was done before I got it. It's not the best paint job but as you can see I take good care of it.<P> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=99075&a=723595&p=15436465&Sequence=2"> <P><BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||14
Z||000013||theogre||08-24-2000||11:51 AM||||peterh,<P>I wouldn't try just shooting a new clear. By the time you get rid of all the nicks in the paint..... Even if there where no nicks thru the paint, the paint layer is so thin that if you hit it at all you'll be thru to substrate before you even know it.<P>There are a number of single stage paints that are really good stuff. You don't have to shoot base and clear to get increadible finishes. Single stages also don't have some of the maintenance problems clear coats have.<P>The big reason car makers love clear coats is that they can shoot diferant parts of the car with diferant base paint formulas and still get a uniform result on the whole car.||||reg||1
Z||000014||stimpy||08-24-2000||10:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by peterh:<BR><B>Is it possible to apply a new coat of clearcoat to an old paint job after sanding with 600 wetdry, etc.? Or will it look like crap?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't know about applying new clearcoat, but IMO, 600 grit is way too coarse to use on a car that you are not going to completely repaint.  have a gold fiero, and anyone who has seen 14 year old GM gold knows that it oxidizs badly. I used 2000 grit wet dry paper, then used the Meguires Clay Bar system, then polished and waxed. My bumpers started off looking like primer, afterwards the silver shined like new. Meguire's rules. Maybe you can get better, but you can never go wrong with Meguires.<P>------------------<BR>Loud Pipes Save Lives!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/stimpy_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||DXR_DAD||08-24-2000||11:23 PM||||lol!!! Stimpy, that last statment: <P><B>"Maybe you can get better, but you can never go wrong with Meguires".</B><P>Do you work for them??  That was good  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||1
Z||000016||peterh||08-25-2000||02:23 PM||||Stimpy,<BR>Do you have any before and after pictures of your car using the 2000 wetdry and the clay bar system? I am interested in trying it.<BR>Thanks for info.<P>||||reg||1
